    "title": "Common Rock-Forming Minerals — Engineering Relevance",
    "type": "reference",
    "data": {
      "headers": ["Mineral", "Mohs Hardness", "Engineering Note"],
      "rows": [
        ["Quartz", "7", "Very hard, abrasive, chemically stable. Dominates sandstone, quartzite, granite."],
        ["Feldspar (K-spar, plagioclase)", "6", "Weathers to clay minerals. Key weathering indicator in granites."],
        ["Mica (muscovite, biotite)", "2.5–3", "Low shear strength along cleavage. Causes slabbing in metamorphics."],
        ["Calcite", "3", "Soluble in acidic water → karst. Defines limestone/marble."],
        ["Dolomite", "3.5–4", "Similar to calcite but slower dissolution."],
        ["Clay minerals", "1–2", "Very low hardness, absorb water, swell. Products of feldspar weathering."],
        ["Gypsum", "2", "Soluble. Problematic in foundations (dissolution voids)."],
        ["Pyrite", "6–6.5", "Oxidizes to sulfuric acid → sulfate attack on concrete, acid rock drainage."]
